# ⭐ THE VOID-RETURNING CALLABLE AT A COMBINATOR, ACROSS EVERY SPELLING OF THE
# CALLEE. An indirect call's fn-pointer CAST *is* the callee's signature. When
# the combinator adapter typed that cast from the RECEIVER instead, a
# `Callable[void(T)]` at `Option[String].map` was called through a
# `Str(*)(void*, void*)` pointer; under the SysV x86_64 large-struct return
# convention the hidden `sret` pointer takes the first integer register and
# every declared argument shifts one slot, so the adapter received the
# environment where its first user argument belongs.
#
# ⚠ ARCHITECTURE IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT IS WHY THE CLASS SAT UNSEEN.
# AArch64 returns a large composite through the DEDICATED `x8` register, so a
# sret pointer never displaces `x0`/`x1` and these cells are green there
# whatever the cast says. Every measurement below is linux/amd64 — the
# architecture CI runs.
#
# AXIS SAMPLED — how the void callee is SPELLED at the combinator argument.
# The adapter has one arm per spelling and they are an enumerated set, so one
# cell is an anecdote (Core #12): the `Callable` PARAM spelling was the only
# one covered, and the two siblings failed in two DIFFERENT ways.
#
#   MEASURED PRE-FIX, per cell, by reverting the fix and rebuilding:
#     callable param   (`combinator_callable_param_same_type.gg`'s `void_map`)
#                      rc 139, SIGSEGV, plain `gg build`, no sanitizer
#     closure value    rc 139, SIGSEGV, plain `gg build`, no sanitizer
#     top-level fn ref BUILD-FAIL, `error: void value not ignored as it ought
#                      to be` — a C-compile failure, not a crash, which is why
#                      a crash-shaped search would have missed it
#
# The three cells therefore pin three distinct PARTIAL reverts: dropping the
# `unit` branch from the `CallableParam` arm reddens cells 1 and 2, and
# dropping it from the `FuncRef` arm reddens cell 3.
#
# ⊕ WHAT THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT DECIDE. What a void `map` EVALUATES to is an
# open owner question (`todo/t0729`) — these cells discard the result, exactly
# as `void_map` does, so they pin the CALL and not the result type. Calling a
# `void` function through a `Str`-returning function pointer is undefined
# behaviour under every answer to that question.
#
# Spelled with TOP-LEVEL functions and a `String` built at runtime: a closure
# literal leaks its 8-byte environment (`todo/t0526`) and this is a top-level
# fixture, so `scripts/sanitize_sweep.sh` sweeps it and its leak allowlist is
# shrink-only. Cell 2 needs a closure VALUE, so it binds the top-level `shout`
# by name rather than writing a literal.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void shout(String s):
    print(mk(s, "!"))

# Cell 1 — the callable PARAMETER spelling: the local's type is erased, and the
# declared return lives on the callable sidecar.
void via_callable_param(Callable[void(String)] f):
    Option[String] o = Some(mk("par", "am"))
    o.map(f)

# Cell 2 — the closure VALUE spelling: a `Callable[T]` local bound to a
# function reference. Same erased local type, reached through the same arm.
void via_closure_value():
    Callable[void(String)] f = shout
    Option[String] o = Some(mk("val", "ue"))
    o.map(f)

# Cell 3 — the top-level FUNCTION REFERENCE spelling: the callee is a
# `FuncRef` constant, so its signature is in `fn_sigs` and the arm that reads
# it must still branch on `unit`.
void via_func_ref():
    Option[String] o = Some(mk("fun", "cref"))
    o.map(shout)

void main():
    via_callable_param(shout)
    via_closure_value()
    via_func_ref()
